wishnode.net — intake phext (phexts/intake.phext): the CLAIMS REGISTER host for the “Ask about a pilot” form. This phext holds NO page copy and NO submissions. It holds the scheme (chapter 1) and, at runtime, one claim record per form submission (chapters 2+). A claim points to the citizen phext that holds the submission itself; citizen data never lives here. Seeded 2026-11-07 with scheme scrolls and an empty register root. No fake claims. Task 34.6.6/7.7.10/7.6.10.REGISTER ROOT — state: EMPTY. claims_written: 0. The backend appends the first claim at the coordinate derived from its tracking code (see derivation scroll). Nothing at or beyond chapter 2 exists yet, by design.CLAIM RECORD FORMAT — overview. One claim = 6 field scrolls at consecutive scroll offsets from a derived base coordinate, plus append-only transition records from offset +10. Fields: +0 header, +1 citizen_id, +2 citizen_pointer, +3 content_hash, +4 created_at, +5 state. Each field scroll is one line of the form `key: value`. The header scroll is `claim: <tracking_code>`.FIELD +1 citizen_id. Opaque ID derived by the server at submission time (never the submitter's name or contact detail — the form collects none). Format: `citizen_id: cz-<16 lowercase hex>`. It names the citizen phext; it reveals nothing about the person.FIELD +2 citizen_pointer. Format: `citizen_pointer: citizen/<citizen_id>.phext @ <coordinate of the submission's first field scroll>`. The pointer is the only link from this register to submission content; the content itself stays in the citizen phext.FIELD +3 content_hash. Format: `content_hash: sha256:<64 lowercase hex>` — SHA-256 of the canonical concatenation of the submission's field scroll texts in coordinate order. Lets any reader verify the claim matches the citizen-phext content without trusting this register.FIELD +4 created_at. Format: `created_at: <RFC 3339 UTC timestamp>`. Written once, at claim creation. Never edited; corrections are transition records.FIELD +5 state. Format: `state: <current workflow state>` — a cache of the latest transition for fast reads. It is written once at creation (state: received). After that it is NEVER overwritten: the authoritative state is the highest-offset transition record; readers must derive state from transitions, and may treat +5 as the creation state only.TRANSITION RECORDS — append-only. Each state change appends ONE new scroll at base+10, base+11, base+12, ... in order. Format: `transition: <from_state> -> <to_state> | actor_role: <role> | at: <RFC 3339 UTC> | note: <short text>`. No scroll in a claim is ever edited or deleted. Roles: submitter, intake-staff, reviewer, system. Only the transitions listed in the workflow scroll are legal; the backend rejects any other move and writes nothing.WORKFLOW — states: received, routed, in_review, closed. Legal transitions: received->routed, routed->in_review, in_review->closed. Same state machine as the Forms Router app. A claim is born in state received (creation writes fields +0..+5 only).ADDRESSING DERIVATION — exact. Given tracking code CODE (the code shown to the submitter; case-insensitive, trimmed, upper-cased before use): h = sha256_hex('wishnode-claim:' + CODE). chapter = 2 + (int(h[0:5],16) mod 800). section = 1 + (int(h[5:10],16) mod 900). base_scroll = 1 + (int(h[10:15],16) mod 880). Claim fields live at 1.1.1/1.1.1/<chapter>.<section>.<base_scroll + offset>. Anyone holding the code can recompute the address with any SHA-256 tool. Collisions: two codes could in principle derive to the same base; the backend checks the +0 header scroll before writing — if it names a different code, it probes base+20, base+40, ... (same chapter/section) until an empty base is found, and records the probe count in the header. Lookup applies the same probe walk.WORKED EXAMPLE (scheme illustration, not a real claim): for a code whose hash begins 3f9a1..., chapter/section/base are computed exactly as in the derivation scroll. The build script prints one fully worked example into docs/COORDINATE-MAP.md using tracking code TEST-0000-EXAMPLE.ROLES AND CLAIMS — who may do what. submitter: create a claim (via the form) and read own claim state by tracking code. intake-staff: append routed transitions. reviewer: append in_review and closed transitions. system: append transitions for timed actions only. Page phexts are published content, readable by all roles; citizen phexts are readable by their citizen (by tracking code) and by staff roles; this register's scheme (chapter 1) is public, claim chapters are staff-readable and submitter-readable for their own derived address only. Enforcement is by the backend checking the caller's role claim on each request; the phexts themselves hold no secrets.RETENTION — claims and transitions are never deleted by the workflow. Closure is a state, not a removal. Any future purge policy must be a new scheme scroll here plus a transition record per affected claim, never a silent edit.